WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:03.069
-Welcome back to "Late Night."
We're here with David Duchovny.

00:00:03.069 --> 00:00:04.103
You've been here in the past
talking about

00:00:04.103 --> 00:00:05.138
your work writing novels.

00:00:05.138 --> 00:00:06.639
-Yeah.

00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:09.308
-But this is your first graphic
novel, which is very exciting.

00:00:09.308 --> 00:00:10.943
This sort of thing
is right up my alley.

00:00:10.943 --> 00:00:12.812
-Yeah.
-Is it up yours, as well?

00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:14.113
Is this something
you always thought --

00:00:14.113 --> 00:00:16.783
[ Laughter ]
I don't know, guys.

00:00:16.783 --> 00:00:19.486
-He's a professional!
-No, that was an accident.

00:00:19.486 --> 00:00:20.686
It's just David
brings it out in me.

00:00:20.686 --> 00:00:24.290
-That's like, "Did you see that
stuff b'low me?"

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:26.192
-Oh, my God. Stop it.
-Okay, I'm sorry.

00:00:26.192 --> 00:00:29.396
-We're trying to sell
this cartoon.

00:00:29.396 --> 00:00:31.597
-Cartoon?
-Cartoon. Now it's a cartoon.

00:00:31.597 --> 00:00:35.167
It's a graphic novel.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:37.103
It's science fiction.
-Yes, it is.

00:00:37.103 --> 00:00:38.571
-Was that something
you wanted to revisit?

00:00:38.571 --> 00:00:40.173
-Not at all.
-Okay.

00:00:40.173 --> 00:00:42.374
-No, I was trying to stay away
from that for so many years.

00:00:42.374 --> 00:00:45.311
But it was -- You know,
if I have an idea, you know,

00:00:45.311 --> 00:00:48.848
that idea will drive me
into the area where it lives.

00:00:48.848 --> 00:00:51.918
So, I mean,
I had this kind of notion

00:00:51.918 --> 00:00:53.252
after I read that book
"Sapiens."

00:00:53.252 --> 00:00:54.387
Did you ever read that book?
-Yeah.

00:00:54.387 --> 00:00:56.489
-Wonderful book. I recommend.

00:00:56.489 --> 00:01:00.126
And, you know, just realizing
there were all these types of

00:01:00.126 --> 00:01:02.695
hominids on this planet,
along with Homo sapiens,

00:01:02.695 --> 00:01:04.564
along with our breed.

00:01:04.564 --> 00:01:07.199
And what happened to them,
and why did we outcompete them?

00:01:07.199 --> 00:01:09.369
"Outcompete" being another word
for eradicate them.

00:01:09.369 --> 00:01:10.570
-Yeah.

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:13.139
-And so I thought,
"What about a fresh planet

00:01:13.139 --> 00:01:14.473
where there were hominids?

00:01:14.473 --> 00:01:18.077
And if we had the chance to be
stewards of that planet,

00:01:18.077 --> 00:01:21.080
would we screw it up the way we
screwed up this planet?"

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:22.248
It's a comedy.

00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:24.650
[ Laughter ]

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:26.619
-I want to say his name right.
Phillip Sevy?

00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:27.753
-Sevy, yeah.

00:01:27.753 --> 00:01:29.455
-So you're working with,
you know, an artist.

00:01:29.455 --> 00:01:31.157
These are incredible
collaborations

00:01:31.157 --> 00:01:32.491
when you do a comic like that.

00:01:32.491 --> 00:01:36.462
Did you find it hard to sort of
write to the format?

00:01:36.462 --> 00:01:38.564
-Yeah, it's very stringent.
There's not a lot of words.

00:01:38.564 --> 00:01:42.702
As a novelist, I can kind of
luxuriate and spread out.

00:01:42.702 --> 00:01:45.304
But, you know,
you get a little balloon.

00:01:45.304 --> 00:01:46.405
You just get a balloon.

00:01:46.405 --> 00:01:48.974
I was like,
"I need a bigger balloon!"

00:01:48.974 --> 00:01:51.210
So, you get these balloons that
you got to fit the dialogue in

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:54.881
and fit, you know, the scene
descriptions in, so it's tough.

00:01:54.881 --> 00:01:56.683
You got to be really economical.

00:01:56.683 --> 00:01:59.118
-I will say, the really tough
part, with all due respect,

00:01:59.118 --> 00:02:00.586
because I've been on
your side, as well --

00:02:00.586 --> 00:02:01.720
I wrote one of these.

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:03.422
And then you write it,
and, again,

00:02:03.422 --> 00:02:05.591
you have to be economical
and you take your time,

00:02:05.591 --> 00:02:08.093
but then the artist really
has to do the hardest part.

00:02:08.093 --> 00:02:09.896
And the fact that you
put it in their good hands --

00:02:09.896 --> 00:02:12.698
And I don't know if you had
the same reaction that I've had

00:02:12.698 --> 00:02:14.500
the same times I've had
something animated or drawn.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:16.569
You cannot believe
how much more they add to it.

00:02:16.569 --> 00:02:17.937
-Oh, I know, because,
well, first of all,

00:02:17.937 --> 00:02:20.973
I can't even draw a stick
figure, so I'm just amazed.

00:02:20.973 --> 00:02:24.010
But Phillip Sevy --
the big hurdle for us was,

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:26.378
on this planet Kepler,
which is a Goldilocks planet --

00:02:26.378 --> 00:02:27.980
Do you know what that is?

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:31.584
It occupies a zone from the sun,
whatever sun it's orbiting,

00:02:31.584 --> 00:02:33.119
that might sustain life.

00:02:33.119 --> 00:02:35.455
Like, the Earth is a gold--
It's not too hot, not too cold.

00:02:35.455 --> 00:02:37.222
Goldilocks.
-Ah, there we go.

00:02:37.222 --> 00:02:38.491
-Yeah, got there.
-You got there.

00:02:38.491 --> 00:02:42.662
-Whew! So, with this,
we had Neanderthals

00:02:42.662 --> 00:02:45.164
or a type of Neanderthal
on this planet.

00:02:45.164 --> 00:02:46.332
And we were always like,

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:47.633
"It can't be like a GEICO ad.

00:02:47.633 --> 00:02:50.970
You know, we want it to be --
So, we spent a long time

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:52.938
figuring out the look,
which is not so different

00:02:52.938 --> 00:02:54.240
from us Homo sapiens.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:55.708
Neanderthals
are our close cousins.

00:02:55.708 --> 00:02:58.144
So Phillip and I spent
many a time

00:02:58.144 --> 00:03:00.346
just kind of figuring out like,
"What is that look

00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:01.980
for the 'thal?"
is what we call them.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:03.082
-The 'thals?
-Yeah, the 'thals.

00:03:03.082 --> 00:03:05.350
Yeah, you like that?
-Yeah, it's great.

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:08.520
Because I don't think they had
slang back then, so...

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:10.323
And then, the last thing
I want to ask you --

00:03:10.323 --> 00:03:12.858
you wrote a book
"Bucky [Bleep] Dent"

00:03:12.858 --> 00:03:14.160
that you just then
directed a film of.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:15.327
-Yes.

00:03:15.327 --> 00:03:17.297
-How is it adapting
your own work?

00:03:17.297 --> 00:03:19.631
-Oh, it was hellish, you know?
-Yeah.

00:03:19.631 --> 00:03:22.635
-It's interesting.
I mean, you know, I've lived it.

00:03:22.635 --> 00:03:25.304
It's in me for so long
that I felt like I knew it.

00:03:25.304 --> 00:03:29.475
But Logan Marshall-Green,
who was the actor in the film --

00:03:29.475 --> 00:03:32.612
he had read the book
while we were doing the movie.

00:03:32.612 --> 00:03:34.046
And he would come to me
with stuff like,

00:03:34.046 --> 00:03:35.180
"You should put this back in.

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:36.382
Why did you cut this out?"

00:03:36.382 --> 00:03:39.652
And I was like,
"Yeah, that is really good.

00:03:39.652 --> 00:03:41.821
Why did I cut that out?"

00:03:41.821 --> 00:03:44.190
So then you start to
second-guess yourself, you know?

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:46.425
-Well, that will be very
exciting to see, as well.

00:03:46.425 --> 00:03:47.460
-I'll come back
to talk about it.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:48.494
-I hope so.

00:03:48.494 --> 00:03:49.929
I'm always thrilled
to have you here.

00:03:49.929 --> 00:03:51.130
Thanks so much for making
the time for us, as always.

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:52.531
-Thank you.

00:03:52.531 --> 00:03:56.368
-"The Estate" is in theaters now
and on demand November 22nd.

00:03:56.368 --> 00:03:58.671
And "Kepler"
is available December 27th.

00:03:58.671 --> 00:04:00.373 line:0%
We'll be right back with
Sarah Jones.

